{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}

{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>AI Assistant</h1>
    <p>
        Rhapsody can answer questions about itself. The assistant searches the documentation that ships inside the framework package, shows you the sections that match, and, during development with an API key, writes a short answer that points back to the pages it used. It is meant to shorten the learning curve of a new framework: instead of hunting through the docs, you ask in plain English and get the page that explains it.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4">
        <strong>Optional and development-only.</strong> Searching the docs from the terminal needs no API key and no network. Written AI answers and the browser page only run when <code>app_env</code> is <code>development</code>. Nothing here is active in production.
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>Ask from the terminal</h2>
    <pre class="command-line"><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody ask "what does the ask command do"</code></pre>
    <p>
        With no API key, the command prints the best matching documentation sections, with code samples, followed by a <code>Sources:</code> list of the pages they came from. With a key (in development), you first get a short answer that cites those sections as <code>[1]</code>, <code>[2]</code>, and so on, and the sources it used are marked <em>cited</em>.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">Answer text that cites the sections it used [1].

Sources:
  [1] Page › Section heading - /docs/page (cited)
  [2] Another page › Another section - /docs/another-page</code></pre>

    <table class="min-w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
            <tr class="text-left border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
                <th class="pr-6 py-2">Option</th>
                <th class="py-2">What it does</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>--no-ai</code></td><td class="py-1">Never call the model, even in development with a key. Just show the matching sections.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>--top=N</code></td><td class="py-1">How many sections to retrieve, from 1 to 10. The default is 4.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>--json</code></td><td class="py-1">Print the result as JSON (question, answer, notices, and every source with its score and text), for scripts and editors.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <hr>

    <h2>Ask from the browser</h2>
    <p>
        The same assistant is available as a page, <code>AskController</code>, which is easier to read than a long terminal answer. Register two URLs in <code>routes/web.php</code>. The page posts to its own path plus <code>/query</code>, so it works at whatever address you choose:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Controllers\AskController;

Router::get('/chat', [AskController::class, 'index']);
Router::post('/chat/query', [AskController::class, 'query']);</code></pre>
    <p>
        Each question shows its answer (when AI is available) and the matching sections as collapsible cards with a link to the full page. The page is a development tool: both URLs respond with a 404 unless <code>app_env</code> is <code>development</code>, so it is safe to leave registered when you deploy.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>How an answer is produced</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Search.</strong> Your question is matched against a search index of the Rhapsody documentation that ships with <code>arout/rhapsody-core</code>. It is a plain keyword search (BM25) that runs locally in PHP: it understands class names such as <code>SchemaOrg</code>, method names, and <code>snake_case</code> or <code>camelCase</code> words, and it weights headings above body text.</li>
        <li><strong>Answer (optional).</strong> In development, with an API key, the top sections are sent to the model with an instruction to answer only from them and to cite each claim as <code>[n]</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Check.</strong> If the answer cites a section that was never provided, or cites nothing at all, it is thrown away and you get the sections instead. If the model says the sections do not contain the answer, you are told the docs may not cover it.</li>
        <li><strong>Sources.</strong> The pages used are always listed, so you can verify any answer against the documentation.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>If anything fails, you still get the docs</h3>
    <p>
        A missing or rejected key, a rate limit, a timeout, a provider outage, a blocked or empty reply: each one produces a one-line notice and the matching documentation sections. The assistant never fails just because the AI is unavailable.
    </p>

    <h3>Docs gaps are recorded</h3>
    <p>
        When the search finds only a weak match, or the model reports that the docs do not cover a question, the question is appended to <code>storage/logs/doc-gaps.jsonl</code> with the best page found and its score. Each line is one JSON object, so the file shows where the documentation needs work.
    </p>

    <h3>What leaves your machine</h3>
    <p>
        Only your question and the text of public documentation sections are sent to the model. No request data, headers, session, environment values, or application code are included.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Setup</h2>

    <h3>1. Get an API key (optional)</h3>
    <p>
        Searching the docs works without one. For written answers, generate a free key at <a href="https://aistudio.google.com/app/apikey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google AI Studio</a> and add it to <code>.env</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key-here
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash</code></pre>

    <h3>2. Check the config</h3>
    <p>The <code>ai</code> block in <code>config.php</code> selects the provider. Gemini is the default:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">'ai' => [
    'provider' => $_ENV['AI_PROVIDER'] ?? 'gemini',
    'gemini' => [
        'api_key'         => $_ENV['GEMINI_API_KEY'] ?? '',
        'model'           => $_ENV['GEMINI_MODEL'] ?? 'gemini-2.5-flash',
        'timeout'         => (int) ($_ENV['GEMINI_TIMEOUT'] ?? 60),
        'connect_timeout' => (int) ($_ENV['GEMINI_CONNECT_TIMEOUT'] ?? 5),
    ],
],</code></pre>
    <p>
        There is nothing to bind: the framework builds the AI client from this block the first time something asks for it, so an app that never uses AI pays nothing. A missing key is not an error until a call is made.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Keeping the search index current</h2>
    <p>
        The index ships in the package as <code>resources/docs-index/chunks.json</code>, so apps need no build step. If you are working on the framework itself and edit a documentation page, rebuild it:
    </p>
    <pre class="command-line"><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody docs:build
php rhapsody docs:build --check   # exits with an error if the index is stale</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Using the AI client in your own code</h2>
    <p>
        The assistant is built on <code>AiClientInterface</code>, which you can type-hint in any container-resolved class. It has two methods, and both return an <code>AiResponse</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\AiClientInterface;

class SummaryController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(protected AiClientInterface $ai) {}

    public function summarize(string $text): string
    {
        $response = $this->ai->generateContent('Summarize: ' . $text, [
            'system'      => 'Reply in two sentences.',
            'temperature' => 0.2,
            'max_tokens'  => 200,
        ]);

        return $response->getText();
    }
}</code></pre>

    <table class="min-w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
            <tr class="text-left border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
                <th class="pr-6 py-2">Option</th>
                <th class="py-2">Meaning</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>system</code></td><td class="py-1">Standing instructions, kept apart from the user prompt. Every provider implementation must honor it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>model</code></td><td class="py-1">Override the configured model for this call.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>temperature</code>, <code>top_p</code>, <code>top_k</code></td><td class="py-1">Sampling controls.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>max_tokens</code></td><td class="py-1">Maximum length of the reply.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="pr-6 py-1"><code>timeout</code></td><td class="py-1">Request timeout in seconds.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        For a conversation, pass an ordered list of turns to <code>generateChatContent()</code>, each with a <code>role</code> of <code>user</code> or <code>assistant</code> and a <code>content</code> string.
    </p>

    <h3>The response</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$response->getText();         // string: the generated text
$response->getFinishReason(); // ?string: 'STOP', 'MAX_TOKENS', 'SAFETY', ...
$response->wasTruncated();    // bool: cut off by max_tokens
$response->wasBlocked();      // bool: stopped by the provider's safety filters
$response->getUsage();        // token counts
$response->getRaw();          // the full provider payload</code></pre>
    <p>
        A truncated or blocked response is a successful call, not an exception, so check those two flags when it matters.
    </p>

    <h3>Errors</h3>
    <p>Failures are thrown as exceptions that all extend <code>AiException</code>:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>AiAuthenticationException</code>: the key is missing, invalid, or not authorized.</li>
        <li><code>AiRateLimitException</code>: quota exceeded. <code>$e->retryAfter</code> holds the wait in seconds when the provider gives one.</li>
        <li><code>AiTimeoutException</code>: the connection or the response took too long.</li>
        <li><code>AiServerException</code>: the provider failed (HTTP 5xx).</li>
        <li><code>AiModelUnavailableException</code>: the configured model name is invalid or retired.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Ready-made JSON endpoint</h3>
    <p>
        <code>BaseAiController</code> exposes <code>generate()</code> and <code>chat()</code> as JSON endpoints with every exception above mapped to an HTTP status. Extend it for app-specific behaviour. If you add a route, keep <code>->middleware('auth')</code> on it unless you deliberately want anyone who finds the URL to spend your API quota.
    </p>

    <h3>Using a different provider</h3>
    <p>
        Implement <code>AiClientInterface</code> and rebind it in your app's <code>bootstrap.php</code>. It is on the list of core bindings an app may replace, and the assistant picks up your client automatically:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">use Rhapsody\Core\Bootstrap\ServiceRegistrar;
use Rhapsody\Core\Contracts\AiClientInterface;

return static function (ServiceRegistrar $services): void {
    $services->singleton(AiClientInterface::class, fn () => new MyOtherProviderClient());
};</code></pre>

    <h3>Testing</h3>
    <p>
        <code>GeminiClient</code> depends on Guzzle's <code>ClientInterface</code>, so tests can use a <code>MockHandler</code> and never touch the network. See <code>tests/Unit/GeminiClientTest.php</code> and <code>tests/Unit/Docs/</code> for working examples, including the assistant's citation checks.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
